<Project>
  <!--
    Works around a .NET SDK restore bug (dotnet/sdk#46288, #42722): for a multi-targeted
    Microsoft.Testing.Platform Exe project (TestingPlatformDotnetTestSupport/OutputType=Exe come
    from TUnit's own imported .props, at Microsoft.NET.Sdk.targets(799,5)'s _CreateAppHost target),
    some SDK feature bands (confirmed broken: 10.0.401; confirmed fine: 10.0.300) never issue the
    implicit PackageDownload for the native apphost template on the *non-primary* TargetFramework —
    net9.0 here, since net10.0 is the SDK's own version and needs no restore at all. Restore then
    completes with no error, and the later build fails with:

      MSB3030: Could not copy the file ".../obj/Release/net9.0/apphost" because it was not found.

    Requesting the package explicitly sidesteps the broken implicit trigger. The version can't be a
    fixed literal: each SDK feature band bundles its own exact AppHostPackVersion for net9.0 (9.0.16
    under 10.0.300, 9.0.20 under 10.0.401 — confirmed by testing both), and PackageDownload requires
    an exact match, so pinning one breaks every SDK that expects a different one. AppHostPackVersion
    is *not* an MSBuild property despite the name and despite `-v:diag` printing it as if it were one
    — it only exists as metadata on the SDK's per-TargetFramework <KnownAppHostPack> items declared in
    Microsoft.NETCoreSdk.BundledVersions.props, so it has to be looked up by matching that item's own
    TargetFramework metadata against $(TargetFramework). This lookup must run inside a Target
    (BeforeTargets="CollectPackageDownloads"): a bare top-level ItemGroup evaluates during restore's
    static-graph pass, before the properties/items above are populated in that context, and produces
    a malformed PackageDownload item that fails restore with MSB4181 and no useful message.

    Confirmed via a debug Message task that $(NETCoreSdkRuntimeIdentifier) — unlike
    AppHostPackVersion — *is* a real, correctly-populated property at this point, so it's used
    directly.

    Re-verify this workaround is still needed (and drop it) once the upstream bug is fixed. If a
    third TargetFramework is ever added to the shared net9.0;net10.0 set, this condition needs
    revisiting — it only compensates for net9.0 today.
  -->
  <Target
    Name="_WorkaroundMissingAppHostPackDownload"
    BeforeTargets="CollectPackageDownloads"
    Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net9.0'"
  >
    <ItemGroup>
      <_AppHostPackForCurrentTfm
        Include="@(KnownAppHostPack)"
        Condition="'%(KnownAppHostPack.TargetFramework)' == '$(TargetFramework)'"
      />
    </ItemGroup>

    <Error
      Condition="'@(_AppHostPackForCurrentTfm)' == ''"
      Text="No KnownAppHostPack entry found for TargetFramework '$(TargetFramework)' — the app-host restore workaround in tests/Directory.Build.targets needs updating."
    />

    <ItemGroup>
      <PackageDownload
        Include="Microsoft.NETCore.App.Host.$(NETCoreSdkRuntimeIdentifier)"
        Version="[%(_AppHostPackForCurrentTfm.AppHostPackVersion)]"
      />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>
</Project>
